---
import { repo } from '../data/site';

const reasons = [
  {
    title: 'Nothing to install',
    body: 'One file, standard library only, Python 3.6 or newer. No pip install and no build step to get through before you can look at a file.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Real file names',
    body: 'Bun keeps a list of every file it packed in. unbuned reads that list, so you get names like 0006-cli.js instead of chunk-9fxe9jf7.js.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Output you can trust',
    body: 'The file is written byte for byte, exactly as it was inside the binary. Formatting only changes the layout, never the code.',
  },
];
---

<section class="section" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Built for the moment a Bun app lands on your disk and you just want to read it.</h2>
      <p>
        Most of the time you want the JavaScript, fast, without setting up a whole reversing project
        first. So: clone it, run one command, and get a file you can search straight away.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="three">
      {reasons.map((r) => (
        <div class="plain">
          <h3>{r.title}</h3>
          <p>{r.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="close">
      Please use it for reverse engineering, security research, malware analysis, learning and recovery
      work. Respect the licence of what you are looking at, and the law where you are.
      <a href={repo}>Star the repo</a> if it saves you time.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .three {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 32px;
  }

  .close {
    margin: 44px 0 0;
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    max-width: 74ch;
  }
</style>
